### Logical Relationships & Justifications

| Parent Goal | Child Goals/Tasks | Relation | Runtime Annotation | Justification |
|-------------|-------------------|----------|---------------------|---------------|
| G1          | G2, G3, G4, G5, G6, G7 | AND | ; (sequential) | The overall delivery process must follow the order: obtain order → fetch meal → decide method → deliver → clean up → open door. |
| G2          | AT0 | AND | – | Obtaining the order is a prerequisite for all subsequent actions. |
| G3          | AT1, AT2 | AND | – | The robot must first navigate to the kitchen and then pick up the meal(s). |
| G4          | AT3 | AND | – | Retrieval capability must be known before deciding how to deliver. |
| G5          | G5.1, G5.2 | AND | FALLBACK(G5.1,G5.2) | If delivering to the table fails (e.g., patient cannot reach), fallback to hand‑over. |
| G5.1        | AT4 | AND | – | Direct delivery to the table is a single action. |
| G5.2        | AT5, AT6, AT7, AT8 | AND | – | Hand‑over requires informing the patient, tracking retrieval, and alerting on errors. |
| G6          | AT9 | AND | – | Dish retrieval is a necessary cleanup step after delivery. |
| G7          | AT10 | AND | – | Opening the door is required to access the room for all tasks. |
| AT9         | – | – | Number of Robots [1,2] | Dish retrieval may be unassisted or require another robot; the range captures both possibilities. |
| AT10        | – | – | Number of Robots [1,2] | Door opening may involve another robot or a human; the range captures both scenarios. |

